{"chunk_id":"R5-264419-001","collection":"ran5_tdoc_chunks","metadata":{"source":["China Unicom"],"tdocNumber":"R5-264419","title":"Discussion on AI-assisted Efficiency Enhancement for 6G Conformance Testing"},"point_id":"17391699668100370346"}
As RAN5 prepares for 6G, the group faces a structural challenge: we must develop 6G conformance test specifications while continuing to maintain the accumulated 5G-Advanced test specifications. The workload is already at a scale that strains the current way of working, and the addition of 6G will increase it further. Analysis of RAN5 meeting TDocList from RAN5#76 (Berlin, 2017) to RAN5#111 (Dalian, 2026) shows that the 5GS_NR_LTE-UEConTest [1] work item alone has produced: - 19,640 TDocs across 36 meetings, of which 17,083 are CRs (87%) - 9,495 CRs agreed (56% of all CRs) - 1,307 discussion papers - Generating 15 new TS/TR and impacted 14 existing TS/TR for RAN5. RAN5 has sustained 1,000-2,000+ TDocs per meeting for nearly a decade. From R16 to R19, 173 5G related UE conformance test work items have been created (31 in R16, 53 in R17, 55 in R18, 34 in R19). A significant portion of these work items is pattern-driven: CADC configurations, HPUE power classes and new bands/BW additions follow well-established templates once the first example test case exists. This repetitive work consumes substantial meeting time and expert effort. The 6G specification modernization study (6GSM, [2][3]) addresses format and tooling aspects such as Markdown and GIT/forge. The RAN5 group has already begun discussing 6G testing and conformance requirements from multiple angles, including test efficiency and simplification [4], WI admission mechanisms [5][6], SIG/RRM overlap reduction [7], 6G RRM requirements [8], PRD21 continuation [9] and SIG readiness [10]. However, AI-assisted workflow efficiency has not yet been formally discussed in RAN5. We believe it should be a complementary track, started now in 5G-Advanced and matured before 6G test specification work begins. The purpose of this paper is to share our observations on where AI-assisted tools can bring the most value to RAN5's work, and to propose a structured way for the group to explore, pilot and evaluate them. We wish to be clear from the beginning: AI-assisted tools are intended to support and augment the expertise of RAN5 delegates, not to replace it. All RAN5 contributions continue to be authored and reviewed by domain experts, and a mandatory human review step applies to any AI-assisted output.

